window.docContents[225].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations":{"contents":"Elliptic equations describe things that have settled down: the steady temperature\nin a room, the electrostatic potential around a conductor, the shape of a soap\nfilm spanning a wire frame. We prescribe the equation inside a domain and data\non its boundary, then ask whether those data determine a solution and how\nregular that solution is.\n\nThere are two approaches. The classical approach studies the\nfundamental solution of Laplace's equation, the mean-value\nproperty, the maximum principle, interior estimates, and representations by\nGreen's functions and Poisson kernels on the ball, the half-space, and bounded\ndomains with  boundary. The variational approach rewrites a\ndivergence-form equation as a bounded coercive bilinear form on\n and solves it with the\nLax–Milgram theorem. Where lower-order\nterms destroy coercivity, the\nFredholm alternative and the weak\nspectrum of the operator describe solvability instead.\n\nRegularity theory connects the two approaches. A weak solution gains Sobolev\nderivatives when the coefficients, forcing, and boundary are regular enough,\nand with smooth data at every order it has a representative smooth on\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"5. Elliptic Equations","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Null-Lagrangians":{"contents":"For a density  of a map , with\n standing for  and  for , the Euler–Lagrange expression in\ncomponent  is\n\n\n\nUsually this expression vanishes only for the critical points of the action\n. A null Lagrangian is a density for which it\nvanishes for every map.\n\nA density \nis a null Lagrangian on  if every smooth map  on \nsatisfies the Euler–Lagrange system, with classical derivatives, at every\npoint of . The determinant density is  on square\nmatrices.\n\nIn every dimension , the Euler–Lagrange expression of the determinant\nvanishes at every point where  is . In\nparticular the determinant is a null Lagrangian on every open set.\n\nThe derivative of  in the entry  is the cofactor\n, at singular matrices as well, and the density\ndoes not depend on . The Euler–Lagrange expression is therefore\n, and the rows of the cofactor\nmatrix of a  map are divergence free. This is the Piola identity. Each\ncofactor is a sum of products of first derivatives, and after\ndifferentiation the mixed second derivatives cancel in pairs.\n\nThe name is explained by what the action does. If the Euler–Lagrange\nexpression vanishes identically, the derivative of the action along any path\nof maps with fixed boundary values is zero, so the action depends only on the\nboundary values.\n\nLet  be a null Lagrangian on an open set  with  joint\ndependence on its arguments. Then every  map on  also\nsatisfies its Euler–Lagrange system. If  is bounded with \ngraph boundary and  has a continuous second jet, then two maps that are\n up to the boundary, continuous on , and equal on\n have the same action\n\nThe Euler–Lagrange expression at a point depends only on the second jet of\nthe map there, and every second jet is realized by a polynomial map, which is\nsmooth. This passes the null property from smooth maps to  maps.\nFor the action, extend  and  across the boundary to  maps on\nthe whole space, using reflection in the graph charts, and consider\n. The first-variation identity writes\n as the integral of the\nEuler–Lagrange expression of  against , plus a boundary term that\nvanishes because  on . The Euler–Lagrange expression\nis zero, so the action is constant in .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009Null Lagrangians and Constraints","header":"9.3.3.1. Null Lagrangians","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--Null-Lagrangians-and-Constraints--Null-Lagrangians"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--The-Trust-Boundary":{"contents":"A certificate includes the mathematical claim and its proof. A QuadratureCert\nincludes its sound field, an AntiderivativeCert includes a checked residual,\nand an EnclosureCert includes the real inequalities connecting its interval\nto the function. Search heuristics, fuel, and subdivision choose which\ncertificate to try; they do not replace these proof obligations.\n\nCAS and bare cega check their computations in Lean's kernel. Their trusted\nfoundation includes the kernel, its type theory, and the axioms used by the\nimported proofs, including propositional extensionality, quotients, and\nclassical choice.\n\nWith native certificates enabled, cega [fast] can instead establish a closed\nfinite computation by compiled evaluation. Its semantic transport remains a\nLean proof, but the generated computation axiom additionally trusts Lean's\ncompiler and runtime. A theorem using that certificate inherits this trust,\nincluding through later lemmas. The kernel profile disables that native route\nand checks the same finite propositions in the kernel. Certificate types\norganize the mathematical guarantees; they do not erase the distinction\nbetween these two ways of checking the computation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Scope, Trust, and Notes","header":"10.10.2. The Trust Boundary","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--The-Trust-Boundary"}});